<h2 id="sources">Where the {{ state.name }} rule comes from</h2>

<ul>
  <li><strong>{{ state.name }} lemon law.</strong> {% if state.lemonLawCitation %}<a href="{{ state.lemonLawUrl }}" rel="noopener">{{ state.lemonLawCitation }}</a>. {% endif %}The attorney general office for {{ state.name }} is listed by the <a href="https://www.naag.org/find-my-ag/" rel="noopener">National Association of Attorneys General</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>Magnuson-Moss, separate from the {{ state.name }} statute.</strong> The Federal Trade Commission explains a warranty versus a service contract in <a href="https://consumer.ftc.gov/articles/auto-warranties-and-auto-service-contracts" rel="noopener">Auto Warranties and Auto Service Contracts</a>. 15 U.S.C. sections 2301 through 2312 do not impose a nationwide repair-count test on {{ state.name | aan }} {{ state.name }} claim.</li>
  <li><strong>Safety recalls are not the {{ state.name }} lemon law.</strong> A no-charge safety repair on {{ state.name | aan }} {{ state.name }} vehicle follows <a href="https://www.nhtsa.gov/recalls" rel="noopener">NHTSA</a> and 49 U.S.C. Chapter 301, including the 15-year first-sale limit in § 30120(g)(1), and an open campaign is not, by itself, {{ state.name | aan }} {{ state.name }} lemon-law case.</li>
</ul>

<p>{{ state.name }} can amend {% if state.lemonLawCitation %}{{ state.lemonLawCitation }}{% else %}its consumer statute{% endif %}. If a number of repair attempts decides the case, read that text or ask the attorney general.</p>

